nd of testing  ourselves and putting our brains through  experiences without having to do them on  our own and more importantly than all we  are kind of shown how when other people  make mistakes they are accepted things  are fine somehow they always end up  working out in the long term while in  school i don't think this is a behavior  that is encouraged accepted or kind of  presented in any shape or form if you're  interested in developing these sort of  skills a book i would definitely  recommend is surely you're joking mr  baineman which is a book about the  physicist richard feynman and he just  messes up so much and makes so many  different mistakes and he's absolutely  hilarious so i would definitely  recommend checking that book out the  second thing i think books teach us so  much better than school is commercial  intelligence and what i mean by this  particularly is that i find it so funny  how no one expects us to kind of  discover electricity from the start  every single time no one expects us to  discover the laws of physics we just  accept that someone else did the work  beforehand they understood something  let's give this information to the new  generation so they can kind of implement  it and take it further no one kind of we  don't discover pythagoras theorem all  over again however when it comes to  emotional intelligence when it comes to  things like understanding the  consequences of trauma or understanding  our own insecurities or understanding  different forms of attachment or the way  that we kind of process our feelings and  think about the world and all this  emotional stuff that is potentially the  biggest thing that is going on in our  life is almost never even touched in  school at all which is absolutely  ridiculous we are brought into the world  shoved into social situations left to  deal with people in like school or  outside of school and left to deal with  our own minds without any sort of  expertise and experience from people in  t